              "title": "Daniel Arovas, Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics, §2.8",
              "url": "https://phys.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Thermodynamics_and_Statistical_Mechanics/Book:_Thermodynamics_and_Statistical_Mechanics_(Arovas)/02:_Thermodynamics/2.08:_Maxwell_Relations"
            },
            {
              "title": "Jeremy Tatum, Heat and Thermodynamics, §12.5, Summary, Maxwell Relations, and Gibbs-Helmholtz Relations",
              "url": "https://phys.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Thermodynamics_and_Statistical_Mechanics/Heat_and_Thermodynamics_(Tatum)/12:_Free_Energy/12.05:_Summary_the_Maxwell_Relations_and_the_Gibbs-Helmholtz_Relations"
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 9,
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "physical_scope": "Equality of mixed response derivatives on positive-temperature C2 single-phase charts where the named thermodynamic potentials are C2 functions of their local natural coordinates; no relation crosses a singular chart or phase boundary.",
        "library": "physics",
        "proof_strategy": "direct"
      },
      {
        "id": "def-td-isentropic-compressibility",
        "kind": "definition",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Isentropic compressibility on a valid local chart",
        "statement": "Fix a constitution $c$, a positive-temperature local energy chart with entropy coordinate $s$ and physical volume $V>0$, and fixed amounts $n=(n_1,\\ldots,n_r)$. The energy representation and pressure on this chart are those of [[pthm-td-local-energy-representation]] and the response-chart conventions are those of [[def-td-response-functions]]. Where $(s,V,n)\\mapsto(s,p,n)$ has a C1 local inverse, define the **isentropic compressibility** of the whole system by\n\n$$\\kappa_S:=-\\frac1V\\left(\\frac{\\partial V}{\\partial p}\\right)_{s,n}.$$\n\nThe entropy $s$ is held fixed, as are all amounts $n_j$. Since $V$ is in $\\mathrm{m^3}$ and $p$ in $\\mathrm{Pa}$ ([[def-td-si-quantities]]), $\\kappa_S$ has units $\\mathrm{Pa^{-1}}$. This definition asserts no sign, finiteness, or existence outside the specified inverse chart; in particular it does not assign a value at a singular or critical response point.",
        "deps": [
          "def-td-si-quantities",
          "def-td-equilibrium-state-domain",
          "def-td-response-functions",
          "pthm-td-local-energy-representation"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-td-si-quantities": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-td-equilibrium-state-domain": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-td-response-functions": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "pthm-td-local-energy-representation": "physical-result"
        },
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Daniel Arovas, Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics, §2.10.5",
              "url": "https://phys.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Thermodynamics_and_Statistical_Mechanics/Book:_Thermodynamics_and_Statistical_Mechanics_(Arovas)/02:_Thermodynamics/2.10:_Applications_of_Thermodynamics"
            },
            {
              "title": "Jeremy Tatum, Heat and Thermodynamics, §13.3, Pressure and Temperature",
              "url": "https://phys.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Thermodynamics_and_Statistical_Mechanics/Heat_and_Thermodynamics_(Tatum)/13:_Expansion_Compression_and_the_TdS_Equations/13.03:_Pressure_and_Temperature"
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 7,
        "inventory_expansion_required": true,
        "physical_scope": "Finite derivative response on a local smooth positive-temperature simple-compressible energy chart at fixed composition; no value or sign is asserted where the inverse chart fails.",
        "library": "physics"
      },
      {
        "id": "pthm-td-reservoir-minimum-principles",
        "kind": "physics-theorem",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Helmholtz and Gibbs potential minimum principles for ideal reservoirs",
        "statement": "Fix a closed macroscopic system with composition fixed and two admitted equilibrium endpoints. In the fixed-volume model, couple it only to an ideal thermal reservoir at constant $T_0>0$. In the fixed-pressure model, also couple it to an ideal pressure store at constant $p_0>0$. Define the constrained potentials\n\n$$F_{T_0}(x):=U(x)-T_0S(x),\\qquad G_{T_0,p_0}(x):=U(x)+p_0V(x)-T_0S(x).$$\n\nAssume the ideal-reservoir bookkeeping specified in [A1]–[A3] of Facts & Assumptions and the adopted isolated-compound second law [[post-td-macroscopic-second-law]]. Then along every admitted spontaneous endpoint change at fixed volume, $F_{T_0}$ does not increase; at fixed pressure, $G_{T_0,p_0}$ does not increase. If an endpoint is an attained global entropy maximum on the same feasible set, the corresponding constrained potential attains a global minimum there. If the system endpoint is on its $T=T_0$ or $(T,p)=(T_0,p_0)$ natural-variable chart, these constrained functions agree with the Helmholtz or Gibbs potentials of [[def-td-legendre-potentials]].\n\nThe statement is conditional on the ideal bath/load model and is not a claim that every physical process has such a reservoir or reaches the minimum.",
        "deps": [
          "def-td-legendre-potentials",
          "post-td-first-law-energy",
          "post-td-macroscopic-second-law",
          "post-td-entropy-maximum",
          "def-td-si-quantities"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-td-legendre-potentials": "formulation-prerequisite",
