        "id": "pthm-td-joule-thomson-chart",
        "kind": "physics-theorem",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Joule-Thomson coefficient on a valid Gibbs chart",
        "statement": "On a C2 Gibbs chart $G(T,p,n)$ at fixed composition $n$, with $T>0$, $V>0$, finite $C_p>0$, and a valid local inverse between $(T,p,n)$ and $(H,p,n)$, define the **Joule-Thomson coefficient** by\n\n$$\\mu_{JT}:=\\left(\\frac{\\partial T}{\\partial p}\\right)_{H,n}.$$\n\nThen\n\n$$\\mu_{JT}=\\frac{T(\\partial V/\\partial T)_{p,n}-V}{C_p}=\\frac{V(T\\alpha_p-1)}{C_p},$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha_p=V^{-1}(\\partial V/\\partial T)_{p,n}$ and $C_p=(\\partial H/\\partial T)_{p,n}$ are the response functions of [[def-td-response-functions]]. This is a local derivative identity. It does not prove that a real throttle is isenthalpic or that a particular substance cools under expansion.",
        "deps": [
          "def-td-legendre-potentials",
          "def-td-response-functions",
